# Forbidden patterns for Ruby files (*.rb, *.rake). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: rb rake
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Patterns are case-SENSITIVE, scanned against the staged blob. Installed as
# `.forbidden-patterns/ruby.txt`.
# Debug rules target breakpoints (binding.pry/irb, byebug, debugger) and pp; a
# second block below targets code-execution and TLS-downgrade sinks. Command
# injection is NOT covered - see the NOT COVERED note in that block.
# Rails.logger and raise are intentionally NOT banned. puts/p are FP-prone
# for CLI tools and ship commented below.
# The `debugger` pattern excludes a trailing `:` so the `debugger:` keyword/hash
# key is allowed; only the bare breakpoint statement is caught.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])binding\.pry($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Remove binding.pry breakpoint before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])binding\.irb($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Remove binding.irb breakpoint before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])byebug($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Remove byebug breakpoint before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])debugger($|[^A-Za-z0-9_:])	Remove debugger breakpoint before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])require[[:space:]]+.(pry-byebug|byebug|pry|debug)('|")	Don't commit a require of a debug gem (pry/byebug/debug)
(^|[^A-Za-z_])save_and_open_(page|screenshot)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Remove Capybara save_and_open_page/screenshot debug call before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])pp([[:space:]]+[^[:space:]=]|[[:space:]]*\()	Remove pp() pretty-print debug call; use the logger instead

# --- Security: code-execution and TLS-downgrade sinks ---
# install.sh ships this file to any repo with a Gemfile - a Rails app, but also
# a plain gem or a CLI - so every rule here has to stay quiet on ordinary
# library Ruby, not just on controllers. Each was first measured against ~16k
# real .rb/.rake files (Ruby 3.1-3.3 stdlib, bundler/rubygems/rubocop/rails/
# devise/sidekiq/haml/liquid gem sources, and the rubygems.org, mastodon and
# forem applications), then re-measured adversarially over a ~34k-file corpus
# that added Discourse and capistrano. That second pass DELETED one rule
# outright and narrowed four more; the notes below describe the rules as they
# now stand, not as they were first written.
#
# eval() is matched in CALL POSITION only - the name must start the statement or
# follow an operator/`(`/`,` - so the declarations `def eval(expr)` and the name
# `evaluate(` do not fire. `Kernel.eval`/`obj.eval` are excluded with them (the
# `.` anchor): a deliberate trade for silence on every method of that name.
# `#`, `-`, a quote and a backtick are excluded as the preceding character, so
# a doc comment (`# eval code`), a CSP directive (`'wasm-unsafe-eval'`, which is
# in every modern Rails content_security_policy initializer), a bare string
# `'eval'` in a method-name list and a markdown `` `eval` `` all stay silent.
# The argument must be a real one - `(` or whitespace-then-non-space - so
# `start_with?("(eval")` does not fire either. The keyword branch (`... and
# eval x`, `return eval x`) carries its own `^[^#]*` anchor, because without it
# ordinary security prose - "do not eval untrusted strings", measured in
# capistrano - fired on the `not eval` in the sentence. Cost of that anchor: an
# `eval` further along a line that already contains a `#` (in a string or an
# interpolation) is invisible to that branch. What is left is irreducible: a
# class that defines its OWN `eval` and calls it unqualified looks exactly like
# the Kernel#eval sink to a regex.
# Marshal.load fires only where its argument is an EXPRESSION -
# `Marshal.load(params[:blob])`, `Marshal.load(request.body.read)`,
# `Marshal.load(@blob)`. A BARE LOCAL VARIABLE is exempt, and so is anything
# starting `Mar` or `::`. That is deliberate: the deep-copy idiom is written
# `Marshal.load(Marshal.dump(x))` but just as often
# `dumped = Marshal.dump(x); Marshal.load(dumped)` (capistrano, twice) or
# `Marshal.load(::Marshal.dump(x))`, and no regex can tell that variable from an
# untrusted one. The earlier `Mar`-prefix-only exemption covered only the first
# of those three spellings and fired on the other two.
# KNOWN HOLE, stated plainly: a load from a variable you filled somewhere else
# is NOT caught. `^[^#]*` keeps doc comments quiet - the un-anchored version
# flagged 7 of them, including stdlib singleton.rb and rubocop's own cop
# documentation.
# YAML: only the explicitly unsafe entry points are banned, and only outside
# comments (`^[^#]*`) - 17 of the 30 measured hits were doc comments, stdlib
# psych.rb among them. Plain `YAML.load` / `YAML.load_file` are SAFE on
# psych >= 4 (Ruby 3.1+), where load is safe_load - banning them would fire on
# ordinary config code in every modern app. Pinned to psych 3.x? Enable the
# opt-in load rule at the bottom of this file.
# send()/public_send() fire only on `params[...]`, `params.fetch(...)`,
# `params.dig(...)`, or a `request.`-derived params hash. `params.<anything
# else>` is NOT enough: `params` is an ordinary local Hash in plenty of
# non-Rails code, and `@channel.send(params.to_json)` has to stay silent. A
# bare `request.` first argument is not enough either: `socket.send(request.to_s)`
# and `client.send(request.body)` are ordinary networking code.
# VERIFY_NONE fires only where the constant is ASSIGNED (`= `, `=>`, `name:`),
# never where it is compared: `if ctx.verify_mode != OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_NONE`
# is a check that verification is ON, and flagging it inverts its meaning.
#
# NOT COVERED - command injection. A rule for a shell string built by
# interpolation (`system("git log #{ref}")`) used to ship here and was DELETED:
# across the ~34k-file corpus it produced 37 hits and ZERO true positives, 21 of
# them in Discourse alone - including commands already escaped through
# `shelljoin`, and a `2>&1` redirect where the "pass an argument array instead"
# fix it demanded is not expressible. Its `^[^#]*` guard was also defeated by
# any earlier `#{...}` on the line, so it was not even reliably disarmed by a
# comment. Nothing in this file flags `system` / `exec` / `spawn` / `IO.popen` /
# `Open3.*` today; use a taint-aware analyzer (brakeman) for that class of bug.
(^[[:space:]]*|[^A-Za-z0-9_.@:#'"`[[:space:]-][[:space:]]*|^[^#]*(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(return|yield|then|else|and|or|not)[[:space:]]+)eval([[:space:]]*\(|[[:space:]]+[^[:space:]])	eval() executes arbitrary code - never build one from input
^[^#]*(^|[^A-Za-z_.])Marshal\.load[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*(([A-LN-Za-z_]|M[^a]|Ma[^r])[A-Za-z0-9_]*[.[(]|[^A-Za-z0-9_:[:space:])])	Marshal.load on untrusted bytes instantiates arbitrary objects (RCE) - use JSON
^[^#]*(^|[^A-Za-z_.])(YAML|Psych)\.unsafe_load(_file)?[[:space:]]*\(	YAML.unsafe_load bypasses psych 4 safe loading and instantiates arbitrary objects (RCE) - use YAML.safe_load with permitted_classes
(^|[^A-Za-z_])(send|public_send)[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*(params[[:space:]]*(\[|\.(fetch|dig)[[:space:]]*\()|request\.(params|query_parameters|request_parameters|GET|POST)[[:space:]]*(\[|\.))	send() with a request-derived method name invokes any method on the receiver - allowlist the name
^[^#]*([^!=<>]=>?|[A-Za-z_]:)[[:space:]]*(::)?OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_NONE	Assigning VERIFY_NONE disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA instead

# Optional (opt-in) - uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])puts[[:space:]]*[\( ]	puts is common legitimate CLI output - enable only if your app uses a logger exclusively
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])p[[:space:]]+[^[:space:]=]	p is FP-prone (single-letter, also a common local var) - opt in with care
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed - open a ticket instead
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(binding\.remote_pry|PryRemote)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Remove remote-pry breakpoint before committing
# Enable the next line ONLY on psych 3.x / Ruby <= 3.0, where YAML.load really is
# the unsafe loader. On psych >= 4 (Ruby 3.1+) load IS safe_load and this fires
# on every ordinary YAML.load(File.read(config)) in the app.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])(YAML|Psych)\.load(_file)?[[:space:]]*\(	YAML.load/load_file instantiates arbitrary objects on psych < 4 - use YAML.safe_load (off by default: safe, and very common, on psych >= 4)
#
# `class_eval`/`module_eval`/`instance_eval` on a STRING or heredoc. Enable in an
# APPLICATION repo only. It is the standard codegen idiom of every library that
# defines methods dynamically: 371 hits across the measured corpus (ActiveRecord
# associations, ActiveSupport, kaminari, ransack, simple_form...), all of them
# benign `class_eval <<~RUBY` with compile-time-known names, and 0 in the three
# real applications. A regex cannot see whether the interpolated name came from
# a user, so on a gem or an engine this blocks nothing but legitimate macros.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(instance_eval|class_eval|module_eval)[[:space:]]*\(?[[:space:]]*(["']|<<[-~]?["']?[A-Z_])	instance_eval/class_eval on a STRING or heredoc executes arbitrary code - define the method literally or allowlist the name
#
# Rails output safety (`.html_safe`, `raw()`) - the ERB twins of the
# dangerouslySetInnerHTML / v-html / {@html} sinks frontend.txt bans. Both ship
# OFF, for two measured reasons. (1) They are POLICY, not defect detection:
# `"".html_safe`, `json_escape(json).html_safe` and `<<~HTML.html_safe` are safe
# by construction, and the three real applications carry 48 `.html_safe` and 4
# `raw()` call sites in .rb files - most already reviewed and marked
# `rubocop:disable Rails/OutputSafety`. Enabling this blocks a first commit on
# dozens of lines its author did not write and cannot fix. (2) `raw` is a common
# ordinary method name outside Rails - REXML (stdlib) calls its own `raw()`.
# Enable them on a greenfield app that has adopted "never mark HTML safe by
# hand, always sanitize", and expect to audit the existing call sites first.
# COVERAGE NOTE: this file scans `rb rake` only, so these two rules never see a
# view. In the measured applications the same sinks appear on 169 lines of
# .erb/.haml against 52 lines of .rb - the majority of the real exposure is in
# templates that no pattern file currently claims.
# \.html_safe($|[^A-Za-z0-9_?])	.html_safe marks a string as trusted HTML - a stored-XSS sink; sanitize instead (the .html_safe? predicate is allowed)
# ^([^#]*[^A-Za-z0-9_.@:#[:space:]])?[[:space:]]*raw[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*[^)[:space:]]	Rails raw() bypasses HTML escaping (XSS) - sanitize it, or render it escaped
